BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Surviving and Living Fully: The River's Response to AIDS
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261205T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261205T180000
UID:261118OjluT9SS3EKJy828UwyFHA
DESCRIPTION:The Russian River Historical Society invites the community to 
 join Surviving and Living Fully: The River’s Response to AIDS\, a free d
 ay of history\, remembrance\, and celebration on Saturday\, December 5th\,
  at Dawn Ranch \\(the original Fife’s\\) in Guerneville. Timed to World 
 AIDS Day\, the event will honor the Russian River community’s extraordin
 ary response to the HIV/AIDS epidemic during the 1980s and 1990s.\n\nAs HI
 V/AIDS devastated LGBTQ\\+ communities across the country\, something rema
 rkable happened along the Russian River. Gay\, lesbian\, and straight neig
 hbors came together to care for one another\, building networks of healthc
 are\, food delivery\, hospice care\, and community support. Their collecti
 ve response became an important example of how rural communities can unite
  in the face of a crisis.\n\nFor the past two years\, the Russian River Hi
 storical Society has worked to document and preserve this largely untold c
 hapter of local history through a California State Library grant. The proj
 ect has created a digital archive of historical records from West County H
 ealth Centers and recorded and transcribed more than 30 oral histories and
  interviews with community members and healthcare providers. The interview
 s include recordings from 2003\, 2005\, and 2026. These materials will be 
 made publicly accessible through a partnership with Sonoma County Library.
 \n\n“Surviving and Living Fully” will bring this history to life throu
 gh walking tours of historic Gay Guerneville\, a special presentation feat
 uring voices from the oral history archive\, and opportunities for communi
 ty members to share their own memories. The presentation will revisit the 
 challenges of the epidemic while highlighting the compassion\, resilience\
 , and collective action that sustained the community.\n\nThe day will begi
 n with small-group walking tours departing from the Guerneville Safeway pa
 rking lot at 11 a.m.\, noon\, and 1 p.m. The main presentation will take p
 lace from 2–4 p.m.\, followed by a hosted reception and dance from 4–6
  p.m. featuring a disco-inspired DJ set by DJ Deks.\n\nThroughout the day\
 , attendees can visit exhibits and community tables hosted by Face to Face
 \, Food for Thought\, West County Health Centers\, Positive Images\, Sonom
 a County Library’s Here & Queer Archive\, and the Russian River Historic
 al Society. Archival photographs\, the original ACT UP banner\, and histor
 ical records from the Russian River Health Center will also be on display.
  A remembrance table will offer space to honor loved ones lost to AIDS and
  share their stories.\n\n“This project is about preserving the stories o
 f the people who showed up for one another during an incredibly difficult 
 time\,” said the project team. “We hope this gathering will honor thos
 e we've lost\, celebrate the community that came together\, and connect th
 ese stories with younger generations.”\n\nThe event is free and open to 
 the entire community\, thanks to generous support from the California Stat
 e Library and community partners. Registration is required. Walking tours 
 require advance registration\, and volunteer opportunities are available.\
 n\nTo volunteer or learn more\, visit russianriverhistory.org or email lgb
 tq@russianriverhistory.org.\n\nAbout the Russian River Historical Society:
  The Russian River Historical Society collects\, preserves\, and presents 
 materials related to the history of the Russian River area and its people.
 \n\nAbout the Project: The project was made possible through the Californi
 a State Library’s Preservation & Accessibility of California’s LGBTQ\\
 + History grant program\, with support from Sonoma County Library\, West C
 ounty Health Centers\, and community volunteers. The resulting digital arc
 hive and oral histories will help ensure this important history remains ac
 cessible to future generations.
LOCATION:Dawn Ranch
URL:http://bit.ly/aids-response
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
